<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oghurs</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Oghurs were a group of Turkic-speaking peoples, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onogurs">Onogurs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saragurs">Saragurs</a> and Ogurs, who moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> onto the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic–Caspian steppe</a> around 463, according to the Byzantine historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscus">Priscus</a>. Their languages formed the Oghuric branch of Turkic, ancestral to that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> and to modern Chuvash. Peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutrigurs">Kutrigurs</a> and Utigurs probably emerged from among them.</p></div>
